        "created_at": "2015-08-24T19:03:45.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I've played Setback a few times and been generally unimpressed. He's got some fun things going on but overall my experience was meh.</p><p>Then we played Legacy and&nbsp;Setback (me) with Ra and Visionary (wife) vs Kaargra Warfang in the block and it all changed.</p><p>Kaargra got an awesome start. First turn she flips herself, second turn she plays the card where she destroys one&nbsp;equipment/ongoing (which is all anyone had) and gets a title, third turn she plays the same card and again destroys all our stuff and gets another title.</p><p>By&nbsp;the end of the third villain turn she&nbsp;has three titles, one where she plays the bottom card of her deck the first time she takes damage&nbsp;each turn, one where she damages all targets 1 psychic the first time she takes damage each turn&nbsp;AND the one where each time she damages a target it get +2 damage taken the next time&nbsp;it gets hit. I admitted it looked grim and suggest we just start over but we decided to play it out it couldn't possible last much longer, what with all our stuff wiped,&nbsp;two villain cards played per turn plus a villain card&nbsp;each hero turn we damage her and crazy damage all over the place.</p><p>At one point she had all but one of her gladiators out and once. Between Legacy's boost and Visionairy and Ra's&nbsp;damage cards we were able to get a pretty good chunk of&nbsp;crowd favor tokens, plus visionary manipulated the crowd by putting fickle fans on the bottom of her deck, so when someone hit Kaargra it swung the crowd in our favor.&nbsp;</p><p>The environment meanwhile played Defessive Displacement Reduce all damage by 2, which then destroyed itself, The Warden, Lock down, Filter Guard and Filter Agent.</p><p>Now to get to Setback, through his first couple turns he was able to build up a good pool of tokens. First turn: Plays and uses Looking up.</p><p>Second turn he risked into Fumbling&nbsp;Fool which discard the top card of each desk, including his own Karmatic Retribution (7 Damage for 7 tokens, dang).</p><p>Third turn Risks Cause and Effect, which does&nbsp;10 damage to himself and Kaargra.</p><p>Fourth turn&nbsp;Risk Whoops Sorry, the card where&nbsp;he destroys&nbsp;another heroes stuff, the only other cards out besides Silver&nbsp;Lining which&nbsp;Setback played this turn and is not an eligible card to destoy, and Legacy's just set up combo of Next Evolution and Lead from the Front, double dang.</p><p>Fifth and Final turn Hero Favor is 15 and Kaargra 16. Kaargra is down to 12 health.&nbsp;Setback is the final hero in turn order. Token pool is up to 12, Setback plays the card that gives him +1 damage to and from villains for each 3 tokens in his pool. Risk...Exceeds Expectations!&nbsp;I remove 5 tokens from his pool leaving 9, to do 5 targets 3+3+1(galvanize) 7 damage. Most villain targets were under Kaargra's +2 damage from her title, including Kaargra herself. It adds 6 tokens to our favor pool for a total of 21 tokens, brings Kaargra to 3 health she hits everyone for 1 damage again incaping Setback but Silver Lining brings him back. Kaargra's at 2 health. Just barely not enough to win the game, and Kaargra is&nbsp;going to get enough tokens on her own turn to win but WAIT. Environment Turn, Kaargra is now&nbsp;tied for lowest health, FILTER guard hit and takes her out. Heroes Win!</p><p>True the environment won the game for us but looking back I see Setback's Fumbling fool on turn 2 discarded a Prision Riot, meaning the FILTER agents were only there because Setback accidently stopped a prision riot which directly led to Kaargra's defeat.</p><p>The text on Exceeds Expectations explains it all \"There's... no way that should have worked.\"</p><p>TL;DR Setback is awesome!</p>",
